<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ranquel</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ranqueles, Rankülche</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ranquel, or Rankülche, are an indigenous people of the central Argentine pampas, formed in the 18th century from Mapuche-speaking and other groups. Living by horse herding and raiding, they were allies and rivals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-calfucura">Juan Calfucurá</a>'s Salinas Grandes confederation and are described in Lucio V. Mansilla's 1870 book An Excursion to the Ranquel Indians. Their independence ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conquest-of-the-desert">Conquest of the Desert</a> of 1878–1879.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ranquel.jpg" alt="Ranquel" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antonio Gritta · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Casa_Ranquel_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Spanish name Ranquel renders their own name, Rankülche, from the Mapudungun rankül, cane, and che, people: the cane people. They are an Indigenous people of the northern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-pampa-province">La Pampa Province</a>, of Puelche, Pehuenche and Günün-a-Küna origins, who were conquered by and assimilated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mapuche">Mapuche</a>.</p><p>Between 1775 and 1790 a group of Pehuenche moved east from the Andes into a country they called Mamül Mapu, the land of woods, for its dense stands of Prosopis caldenia, Prosopis nigra and Geoffroea decorticans. They settled along the Cuarto and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a> rivers, from the south of the present provinces of San Luis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> to the south of La Pampa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the late 18th and early 19th centuries the Ranquel held two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a>. They were nomadic hunters, and for much of the 19th century they were allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehuelche">Tehuelche</a>, travelling with them into western Buenos Aires Province and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, and to Mendoza, San Luis and Santa Fe. In 1833 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-manuel-de-rosas">Juan Manuel de Rosas</a> led the Desert Campaign of 1833–34, which tried to destroy them; under their leader Yanquetruz they defended themselves skilfully, using the open terrain. Painé Guor succeeded Yanquetruz around 1834.</p><p>The Ranquel later sided with the forces of Felipe Varela in his rebellion against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguayan-war">Paraguayan War</a> and the central government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>. Their last chief, Pincén, was confined in the prison on Martín García island in 1880. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conquest-of-the-desert">Conquest of the Desert</a> their numbers fell further and the army occupied their lands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>A reservation, the Colonia Emilio Mitre, was set up for them in what is now La Pampa province, and their descendants live there. On 14 August 2007 the government of San Luis province returned 2,500 hectares to the Ranquel people, including two small lakes, about 124 km south of the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fraga">Fraga</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ranquel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…and then, in the Conquest of the Desert led by General Julio Argentino Roca in 1878–1879 and continued into the mid-1880s, used Remington rifles, the telegraph and railways to overrun the lands of the Mapuche, <b>Ranquel</b> and Tehuelche peoples, killing and displacing thousands and opening Patagonia to settlement.</blockquote><a href="/empire/argentine-republic">Argentine Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Pampas and Patagonia were Mapuche, <b>Ranquel</b> and Puelche country, the Gran Chaco Guaycuru.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-provinces-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">United Provinces of the Río de la Plata · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After eliminating rival Boroano chiefs at Masallé, he built a confederation of Mapuche, <b>Ranquel</b> and other pampas groups.</blockquote><a href="/state/salinas-grandes-confederation">Salinas Grandes Confederation</a></li></ul></section>
